the Renaissance and Baroque periods, many Romance‑language countries (Italy, France, Spain, Portugal, and later much of Latin America) gradually adopted Guido’s solfège syllables **as absolute pitch names**, replacing the older letters. This is **fixed do**: **do** always equals **C**, **re** = **D**, **mi** = **E**, and so on. In these countries, a musician would say *do* for the note C, *sol* for G, etc. The syllable *si* (or *ti* in some English‑speaking methods) was added later for the seventh degree. - English‑speaking and Germanic countries kept the letter system for instrument names, music notation, and theory, while using solfège only as a movable‑do ear‑training tool (or not at all). So you’ll see “C‑D‑E‑F‑G‑A‑B” on piano keys and in books in English, and “do‑re‑mi‑fa‑sol‑la‑si” in French or Italian conservatories. ### In a nutshell | System | Example | Usage | |--------|---------|-------| | Letters (absolute) | C‑D‑E‑F‑G‑A‑B | English‑speaking countries, Germany (with H), etc. – fixed pitch | | Fixed‑do solfège (absolute) | do‑re‑mi‑fa‑sol‑la‑si | Romance‑language countries (Italy, France, Spain…) – these *are* the note names | | Movable‑do solfège (relative) | do‑re‑mi… (do = tonic) | Pedagogical tool in English‑speaking schools; not used as note names | So the representation depends on **geography and musical tradition**: one system grew from ancient Greek letters, the other from a medieval sight‑singing innovation. Both are still in common use today, just in different parts of the world.
